        "descriptions": [
          {
            "descriptionType": "Abstract",
            "description": "This dissertation focuses on the design of antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) against plant pathogenic bacteria using machine learning models based on N-gram representation of peptide sequences. The putative AMPs predicted by the N-gram-based machine learning models were assayed for activity against Spiroplasma citri and P. syringae pv. tabaci. Software with a web interface using N-grams with a 9-letter reduced alphabet was developed to facilitate the identification of artificial AMPs with the potential to target bacterial plant pathogens, including cell wall-less bacteria in the class Mollicutes. The use of AMPs has been explored to combat diseases in plants, since using antibiotics on a wide scale can be cost-prohibitive and lead to antimicrobial resistance. Further, AMPs could be used and engineered into transgenic plants against plant pathogens that affect agricultural crops. However, the identification of AMPs through laboratory assays can be resource intensive, time-consuming, and cost-prohibitive with respect to synthesizing AMPs. Consequently, the development of bioinformatic tools is needed to identify potential AMP candidates in a high throughput manner. This study used a Random Forest-based machine learning approach based on N-grams for predicting AMPs. This study utilized a reduced alphabet and proposed N-grams based on a 9-letter alphabet and a 3-letter alphabet based on charge. The N-gram method achieved a prediction accuracy ranging from 0.72 - 0.91 using a 5-fold cross-validation of the training set and a separate test set. This study also produced positive AMP datasets (based on gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria in the Antimicrobial Peptide database (APD) and a negative dataset that could be useful for further research in AMP prediction. Further, four peptides encoded by the intergenic regions and small ORFs of the citrus genome were identified as putative AMPs using N-gram program. These peptides showed activity in a laboratory assay using a microplate reader against Spiroplasma citri. Peptide stats and characteristics of predicted peptides that showed AMP activity were also explored. The N-gram AMP prediction program AGRAMP, where the user can input FASTA formatted sequences, can be accessed at http://omics.gmu.edu/agramp/index.php."
          }
        ],
